  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nOther than a bit of cirrus, skies are again mostly clear early this \nmorning with light winds. Since conditions are similar to\nyesterday, valley fog will probably develop in some spots, \nwhich could be dense in some spots before clearing out by mid \nmorning. Another area to watch for fog is roughly along and east\nof the I-95 corridor from Portland to Augusta as the HREF probs\ncontinue to have a fog signal. The rest of the day will be \nmostly sunny and mild with temperature ranging from the upper \n50s to mid 60s. The pressure gradient will tighten a bit between\nthe high pressure to our north and the nor'easter off of the \nMid-Atlantic coast, so we could see N-NE winds gusting 20-25 \nmph, especially near the coast and parts of western ME.\n\nHigh pressure builds right back overhead tonight with mostly clear \nskies once again. This will bring a good another good setup for \nradiational cooling and lows in the 20s to lower 30s for northern \nand western areas, which will be closer to the center of the higher. \nFarther south, there may be enough of a breeze to keep the boundary \nlayer more mixed, so I haven't gone quite as cool, but still \nforecast lows are in the 30s.  \n\nDry conditions continue into Wednesday and Thursday, but \nhighs will be a toucher cooler and stay mostly in the 50s for \nWednesday before rebounding into the upper 50s to lower 60s on \nThursday. \n\nGlobal models are in good agreement in an upper trough/low diving \nsouthward out of eastern Canada late Thursday into Friday, and \nthis will keep the nor'easter well to our south and east, but \npossibly close enough for breezy conditions Thursday or Friday. \nThe ECMWF and more of its ensemble members continue to advertise\nmore moisture for the upper low to work with than other \nguidance, and this could bring some light QPF across northern \nand eastern areas with light snow in the mountains and rain to \nthe south. We'll see if other guidance follows this trend. \n\nMore dry weather is in store for the weekend as Canadian high \npressure builds back in, and if the center of the high ends up \noverhead, Friday night has the potential to be quite cold. A front \nmay cross through early next week, but moisture looks very limited, \nand the majority of ensemble guidance favors dry conditions.",
